    "abstract": "The agricultural supply chains (ASCs) in Ukraine face significant challenges due to internal issues and external factors, including COVID-19, economic stagnation, and the full-scale invasion. These challenges have led to supply chain failures, longer shipping times, disrupted interactions, and increased safety risks for personnel and goods. To resolve these challenges, research is needed to analyze the functioning of supply chains, particularly in agricultural enterprises. Developing a model for operational accounting in agricultural product delivery is essential, using parameters such as the number of trucks, harvesting equipment, productivity, service intensity, and safety characteristics. The study aims to develop a decision-support model using Petri Nets (PNs) to facilitate interaction among ASC participants. Key parameters will serve as inputs for modeling, leading to a proposed framework for a Decision-Making Support System (DMSS). This framework can help agricultural enterprises in enhancing operational planning and security management within their ASCs. In the enterprise case, the DMSS defines threshold regimes D1-D5 and provides a fleet-sizing lookup for SLA16 (S ≤ 16 h) and SLA13 (S ≤ 13 h). Shipping time improves from 16.0 h (1 harvester, 10 trucks) to 11.8 h (1 harvester, 50 trucks), but adding more harvesters without sufficient trucking leads to transport-collapse regimes with shipping times of 19.8–20.5 h. Empirical validation over 10 campaign days shows MAE = 0.44 h, RMSE = 0.52 h, MAPE = 2.73%, bias = -0.26 h, and 90% coverage of the 95% simulation interval.",
